💡 **SUGGESTION: `startConfiguredRework` hand-composes the claim→trigger→stamp protocol that `triggerCappedAutoRework` already wraps**

The rework lap built the protocol into `js/config.js` — `checkAutoReworkBudget` (null when capped / needs-human, `round + 1` when within budget) and `stampReworkRound` — and wrapped them in `triggerCappedAutoRework` (config.js:325-337), which `postStoryTestAutomationReview.js` and `retryMergePR.js` now both delegate to. `startConfiguredRework` here re-implements the same composition inline:

```javascript
var nextRound = checkAutoReworkBudget({
    ticket: params.ticket,
    customParams: ctx.customParams,
    tracker: ctx.tracker,
    ticketKey: ctx.ticketKey
});
if (nextRound === null) return null;
var triggered = triggerConfiguredRework(ctx);
if (triggered) {
    stampReworkRound(ctx.tracker, ctx.ticketKey, nextRound);
}
```

That makes **three** implementations of the claim→trigger→stamp-after-truthy shape (`triggerCappedAutoRework`, this function, and `postStoryTestAutomationShared.triggerAutoStartReworkWorkflow`). The stamp-after-truthy semantics and the null contract now have to be kept in sync in three places — the same triplication hazard F3 flagged for the counting logic, one level up.

The only genuinely local pieces are the `pr_approved` skip gate and the `scm: ctx.scm` trigger option — both compose cleanly:

```javascript
function startConfiguredRework(ctx, params) {
    if (!autoStartReworkConfigured(ctx.customParams)) return false;
    if (hasPrApprovedLabel(params.ticket)) {
        console.log('ℹ️ autoStartRework: skipped — ticket has pr_approved label');
        return false;
    }
    return triggerCappedAutoRework({
        autoStart: autoStart,
        ticketKey: ctx.ticketKey,
        ticket: params.ticket,
        customParams: ctx.customParams,
        config: ctx.config,
        scm: ctx.scm,
        tracker: ctx.tracker,
        label: 'pr_rework'
    });
}
```

(`triggerConfiguredRework`'s current options bag is byte-identical to what `buildAutoReworkTriggerOptions` produces for the same inputs, so behavior is preserved. `postStoryTestAutomationShared.triggerAutoStartReworkWorkflow` keeps its injected-helpers seam for the same reason — fine to leave, or it could delegate with the injected budget helpers once `config.js` helpers accept them.)

Not blocking — the behavior is correct and tested; this is API-shape hygiene.

🪶 **NIT: `isFlagConfigured` re-implements the flag check that `triggerConfiguredAutoStartWorkflow` inlines 40 lines above**

Same file, `triggerConfiguredAutoStartWorkflow` (gh-1246 canonical trigger) already owns this exact check:

```javascript
function triggerConfiguredAutoStartWorkflow(autoStart, ticketKey, customParams, config, spec) {
    var configFileKey = spec.flag + 'ConfigFile';
    if (!customParams || !customParams[spec.flag] || !customParams[configFileKey]) {
        return false;
    }
    ...
```

The new `triggerAutoStartReworkWorkflow` needs the configured/capped/not-configured distinction up front, so it asks the same question via a fresh helper:

```javascript
function isFlagConfigured(customParams, flag) {
    var cfgKey = flag + 'ConfigFile';
    return !!(customParams && customParams[flag] && customParams[cfgKey]);
}
```

Two copies of one predicate in one file will drift (e.g. someone tightening one to `=== true`). Since `triggerConfiguredAutoStartWorkflow` already resolves `spec.flag`, the cheapest single source of truth is to have it call `isFlagConfigured(customParams, spec.flag)` internally — the wrapping check in `triggerAutoStartReworkWorkflow` stays, the inlined copy goes.

Pure dedup, nothing behavioral.

